{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Turns ratio equation: Vp / Vs = Np / Ns. Keep primary on top on both sides",
   "Power equation (ideal transformer): Vp × Ip = Vs × Is",
   "Step-up: more secondary turns → higher output voltage, lower output current",
   "National Grid uses step-up to reduce current, minimising power loss (P = I²R)"
  ],
  "title": "Transformers and the National Grid",
  "content": "<p>Two equations — apply them in sequence when both are needed.</p><p><strong>Turns ratio:</strong> Vp/Vs = Np/Ns. If you need Vs, rearrange: Vs = Vp × (Ns/Np). The most common error is inverting the ratio — primary always over secondary on both sides.</p><p><strong>Power balance:</strong> Vp × Ip = Vs × Is. In an ideal transformer, all power in = power out. If voltage goes up, current goes down by the same factor.</p><p><strong>National Grid logic:</strong> Step-up transformer increases voltage → reduces current. Power lost as heat = I²R — small current means very little wasted energy. Near homes, step-down transformers reduce voltage to approximately 230 V a.c. at 50 Hz.</p>"
 },
